I: Let’s kick off with our first topic. Imagine a club website where you can filter clubs by interest, time commitment, and difficulty level to get matched results right away. If you were using these three filters now, could you walk me through your entire search process step by step?
S: Yeah, totally. First off, I’d definitely pick my interests first, like straight away. Because there are so many clubs on campus, I just wanna rule out everything I’m not into at all first, it saves so much time. Then I’d adjust the time commitment filter, I’d set it based on my weekly classes and study time, skip any clubs that take up too much time or have meetings during my busy hours. After that, I’d go for beginner or casual difficulty, I’m not looking for anything super intense or skill-heavy. Once I set all three, I’d quickly scroll through the filtered clubs, glance at their short intros, and click into a few that look nice to check more details before picking a few I like.

I: Among interest, time commitment and difficulty level, which one matters the most to you? Tell me more about why you prioritize it that way.
S: Oh, time commitment is hands down the most important for me. As a student, my class schedule is fixed, and I also need time to finish assignments and relax. Even if a club is super interesting and easy to join, if it clashes with my classes or takes up all my free time, I can’t join it no matter what. Interest and difficulty are flexible— I can try a new hobby or learn a little basic skill, but time is something I can’t just change whenever I want.

I: What little features or design details on the website would make this filtering process really easy and smooth for you?
S: Hmm, first I hope the filter bar is always there on the page, not hidden somewhere hard to find. And when I pick a filter option, the results update right away without reloading the whole page, that’s so annoying when it reloads every time. Also, simple labels, like no confusing words, a slider for time so I can just drag it, and a one-click reset button if I wanna start over. Oh, and saving my previous filter settings would be great too, so I don’t have to set it again next time.

I: Even with these three main filters, do you ever feel unsure or confused while searching? Why does that happen?
S: Yeah, all the time. Because those filters are just surface tags, they don’t show the real situation of the club. Like a club might say low time commitment, but then they have a bunch of extra after-meeting work or weekend events you’re kind of expected to go to. And some beginner-labeled clubs actually need some basic skills you don’t know about. The tags are too vague, so I can never be totally sure just from them.

I: Besides these three core filters, what other info do you need to see to be sure a club is a good fit for you?
S: I wanna know if there’s any membership fee, how often they actually meet, what the current members think about it, and if they help new people fit in. Also, how big the club is— I don’t like super crowded clubs. And if their activities are the same every semester, not just random stuff. Those real, practical details are way more important than the filter tags.

I: Are there any other custom filters you wish the website had to help you find the right club faster?
S: Definitely! I want a filter for membership fees, so I can exclude all clubs that cost money. Also one for club atmosphere, like casual or competitive, and whether it’s friendly to freshmen. Oh, and a filter for activity type— online, offline, or outdoor. Those extra filters would help me narrow it down so much quicker, without having to check every club one by one.

I: Moving on to social-driven features. Suppose the website shows you clearly when your friends join or bookmark a club. How much would that make you click and check that club out?
S: Oh it would make me do it right away! When I search alone, I only look for clubs I already know, I never check those niche ones. But if I see my roommates or classmates are in a club, I’ll definitely click to see what it’s about, even if it’s something I never thought of joining before. I trust my friends’ choices, so I’m way more curious about those clubs.

I: Would you actually join a club just because your friends are in it? Why or why not?
S: No, I wouldn’t join it only for my friends, but it makes me way more likely to apply. Going to a new club alone is so awkward, I don’t know anyone and I feel out of place. But if my friends are there, I can go with them, fit in faster, and have more fun. But the club still has to be something I kinda like— if it’s totally not my thing, I won’t force myself to go just to hang out with them.

I: What social features related to friends would make you log in and use this website more often?
S: I’d like a little feed that shows what clubs my friends joined or what events they’re going to. A share button so I can send a club to my friends directly, and a simple follow button for friends’ club activity. It makes the website more than just a place to search for clubs, it feels more like a little community, so I’d check it more often.

I: Do you think seeing your friends’ club choices would make you try clubs you’d never pick on your own?
S: Absolutely! I always stay in my comfort zone, only joining the same type of clubs. But when I see my friends trying volunteer, art, or sports clubs I never look at, I wanna give it a go too. It pushes me to try new things and makes my campus life more interesting, instead of sticking to the same old stuff.

I: What other fun social features do you want on this club platform?
S: Maybe a comment section where current members can answer questions, a wall to share photos or videos of club activities, and a buddy system that pairs new members with senior ones. Those small features make the club feel more friendly and connected, not just a formal organization, so joining and participating is more fun.

I: Next up is clear club information. Every club page has regular activities, meeting times, entry requirements and real member experiences. What’s the most important detail you need to see before applying?
S: The exact meeting time and if attendance is mandatory. I need to know exactly when they meet, how long each activity is, and if I have to go to every single one. If I don’t know that, I might sign up and then realize it conflicts with my schedule, and I’ll have to quit later, which is a hassle.

I: Do you think current club information online is clear and complete? If not, what’s missing?
S: No, not at all. Most club introductions are just promotional, they only talk about the good parts. They never say specific weekly plans, if there are any hidden costs, how they treat new members, or what the real atmosphere is like. Everything’s too vague, so I’m always guessing what the club is actually like.

I: How does clear, transparent club info help you decide faster and better?
S: It saves me so much time! I can compare different clubs straight away, and I don’t have to message the club leaders a million times to ask basic questions. When everything’s laid out clearly, I don’t hesitate as much, I can tell right away if it’s a good fit and make my decision quickly.

I: What details about club culture and atmosphere should be on every club page for sure?
S: They should say if it’s a relaxed, low-pressure environment, if they welcome introverts and freshmen with no experience, and if it’s more casual, academic, or competition-focused. That’s super important— I need to know if I’ll feel comfortable there, not just what activities they do.

I: What club profile info would make you totally sure it’s the perfect club for you?
S: If they have detailed monthly activity plans, real photos and videos from past events, honest reviews from members, clear fee info, and even the little downsides of the club. When they don’t just brag but show the real deal, I can know for sure if I’ll fit in long-term.

I: Last topic: the joining process. The website has a simple online application, you can finish signing up in a few minutes. How does this easy process affect your motivation to join clubs?
S: It makes me way more willing to join! The old way is such a pain— filling out paper forms, handing them in offline, waiting forever for approval. I usually give up halfway because it’s too much trouble. But if I can do it quickly online, I don’t feel lazy about it, I’ll try more clubs and not be scared of the application process.

I: Alright, that’s all our interview questions. Do you have any other thoughts or suggestions for this club discovery website?
S: Overall, I just hope it’s simple and practical, not full of fancy but useless features. Focus on making filters easy, club info honest, social stuff light, and the application smooth. Solve the real problems we have when looking for and joining clubs, that’s all we need.

I: Thank you so much for your honest and detailed answers, this is really helpful for our website design. We really appreciate your time.
S: No problem at all! It was fun talking about this, I hope my thoughts help your project go well.